sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch11-02> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "A deflecting force of constant direction acting from outside, such as wind or gravity, can only bend a growing structure towards a limiting direction and never into a spiral; a spiral arises only when the deflecting force is intrinsic to the growing body and turns with the direction of growth, as the columellar muscle does in the snail-shell.";
    dc:type "mechanism";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "A spiral needs a deflecting force carried by the growing body, not an external one";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "D’Arcy Wentworth Thompson, On Growth and Form (1st ed., Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1917), Chapter XI, “The Logarithmic Spiral”; topic “Forces producing a spiral”. Scope: spirally growing shells and other growing structures subject to deflecting forces"
}
